WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.320
Listen up, bro: Fans have found a&nbsp;
strange criticism of the dialogue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.020
in The Way of Water — and you'll&nbsp;
never guess what it could be…

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:12.300
Unless you've been living under a giant rock on a&nbsp;
planet far, far, away, you are probably aware that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.980
James Cameron's blockbuster Avatar: The Way of&nbsp;
Water has hit theaters across the United States.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.340
"Back together, 13 years later, pretty exciting."

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:21.780
"Yeah, it is, right?"

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:26.940
The online discussion around the movie has been&nbsp;
mostly favorable, with plenty of praise mixed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.260
with a few critiques — namely, the relatively&nbsp;
thin plot and more than three-hour run time.

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:35.940
The second installment in James Cameron's&nbsp;
planned five-film arc also earned kudos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.800
for the stunning visual effects and its&nbsp;
criticism of Earth's wasteful capitalist ways.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.980
"What does Eywa's heartbeat sound like?"
 
"Mighty."

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:48.300
However, some fans are pointing out&nbsp;
that a particular scripting choice is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.960
at best unintentionally hilarious,&nbsp;
and at worst kinda distracting.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.880
Much of Avatar: The Way of Water centers&nbsp;
on the children of Jake Sully and Neytiri,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.360
as well as the teenagers of&nbsp;
the seafaring Metkayina clan.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.200
While the dialogue seems to be one of the most&nbsp;
criticized aspects of the film in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.920
the frequent usage of the word "bro"&nbsp;
by the male teenage characters had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.880
many viewers cringing in their seats. In&nbsp;
the official discussion thread for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.100
film on the r/movies subreddit, one user wrote:

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.640
"Bro... I lost count of how many&nbsp;
times they called each other bro..."

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.380
Another user observed:

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.080
"That's how teenagers talk. Loved it."

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.800
One Redditor was less gracious,&nbsp;
commenting that the overuse of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.380
"bro" was their biggest gripe&nbsp;
about the movie. They said:

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.240
"Why do they talk like that? They wouldn't have&nbsp;
gotten that from Jake, he is too old to talk like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:37.260
that. Seems like an old man writing characters&nbsp;
to relate to a modern teenage audience."

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:39.960
"How do you do, fellow kids?"

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:46.140
James Cameron is 68 and co-screenwriter Rick&nbsp;
Jaffa is 66, of course, so this user seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.460
spot on with their analysis. Another user was&nbsp;
more accommodating to the choice and wrote:

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.480
"That's actually how people talk.&nbsp;
I'm 28 and I still start a ton of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.940
sentences with 'bro...' when&nbsp;
I'm hanging with my friends."

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.840
While not every commenter volunteered&nbsp;
their ages, those up on more modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:04.980
slang asserted that teens really don't&nbsp;
say "bro" anymore. One Redditor said:

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.500
"'Bruh' is the actual saying these days."

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.980
The point raised about how the teenagers&nbsp;
would have even learned such language is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:14.460
valid. These characters do not have&nbsp;
American pop culture to learn from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:19.260
and it's a bit of a stretch to think that Jake&nbsp;
would have been calling his own sons "bro" while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.060
they were growing up. Though that's not entirely&nbsp;
out of the question, as one Reddit user posited:

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:27.600
"Well they did learn an entire language&nbsp;
from a self-proclaimed jarhead."

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.620
Even if Jake himself didn't instill the&nbsp;
usage of the word "bro" in his kids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:35.640
there are other justifications for&nbsp;
its frequent usage. Jake isn't the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.580
only Earth-born being living&nbsp;
on Pandora among the Na'vi.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:43.620
A few of the scientists featured in the first&nbsp;
film, including fan-favorite Dr. Norm Spellman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.720
also chose to stick around the planet in the wake&nbsp;
of the first battle with the Sky People. Jake's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.040
children and Spider are shown to have a convivial&nbsp;
relationship with these English-speaking humans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.400
so it's possible they could have picked&nbsp;
up on their slang over the years.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.260
That would also explain why the slang feels&nbsp;
dated, if adults like Jake and Norm are their only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.060
reference for American culture. Another potential&nbsp;
explanation: at the beginning of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.920
Jake narrates to the audience that he understands&nbsp;
the Na'vi language so well that it "may as well be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:15.480
English," which essentially becomes a convenient&nbsp;
workaround for the movie's English dialogue.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:16.980
"You do not know the sea."

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:17.880
"We will learn your ways."

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.660
If that's the case, perhaps the English we're&nbsp;
hearing isn't actually what's being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.100
and "bro" is a translation of a more&nbsp;
appropriate Na'vi word. One Reddit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.880
user even attempted to figure out what&nbsp;
the Na'vi equivalent would be, writing:

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:35.220
"I looked up a translator. 'Brother' in Na'vi&nbsp;
is 'Tsmukan.' I cannot decide what I like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.460
thought of more if I were hearing it&nbsp;
in their native language. Mukan, kan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:42.840
tsm, or tsmu ... I like tsmu. It sounds silly."

